<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satrapy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as satrap, satrapal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A satrapy was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> governed by a satrap, a royal governor responsible for taxation, security and administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> organised the empire into satrapies assessed for fixed tribute. The system was adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> and its successors such as the Seleucid and Ptolemaic empires, and the title was also used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Shaka satraps</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> profile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satrapy.jpg" alt="Satrapy" width="200" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Jansoone JoJan · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achaemenid_Satrap_Asia_Minor_end_of_6th_century_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Darius I (r. 522–486 BCE) formalised the economic administration by dividing the empire into <b>satrapies</b>, twenty according to Herodotus, each assessed a fixed annual tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Achaemenid Empire the city paid tribute through the <b>satrap</b> at Sardis and was governed by local leaders acceptable to Persia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Greeks had lived in the northwest of the Indian subcontinent since the Achaemenids relocated rebellious Greek communities there, and Alexander the Great's conquest of 326 BCE left <b>satrapies</b> and cities such as Bucephala behind.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The land became the <b>satrapy</b> of Armenia, held for three centuries by the Orontid dynasty, who after Alexander's victory at Gaugamela in 331 BCE and the brief tenure of the Macedonian general Neoptolemus returned not as satraps but as kings: Orontes III and Mithridates of Lesser Armenia declared…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The oldest 'Colchian' coins, dated to the mid-6th century BCE and long the primary evidence for a Colchian state, have on reassessment turned out to be the production of an Achaemenid <b>satrapy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Lydia became the Achaemenid <b>satrapy</b> of Sparda, with Sardis as the western capital of the Persian Empire and the end of the Royal Road from Susa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
